<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sulmona</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sulmo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sulmona, the ancient Sulmo, is a town in the Peligna valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, originally one of the main towns of the Paeligni. It became a Roman municipium after the Social War and is best known as the birthplace of the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> in 43 BCE. Its medieval centre includes the aqueduct of 1256 and the Annunziata complex.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sulmona.jpg" alt="Sulmona" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lorenzo Testa · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_di_Sulmona.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sulmona lies in the Valle Peligna, a plain once occupied by a lake that disappeared in prehistoric times. In the province of L'Aquila, it was one of the principal cities of the Paeligni, an Italic tribe. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> praises its healthy air and the many permanent streams of clear water in its neighbourhood, and calls it gelidus Sulmo, cold Sulmo, since like the whole district it was extremely cold in winter. The territory was fertile, with grain and wine, and Pliny mentions the care given to the irrigation of the vineyards of one district, the Pagus Fabianus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Nothing is recorded of Sulmo before the Roman conquest. A tradition alluded to by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silius-italicus">Silius Italicus</a> gave its foundation to Solymus, a Phrygian companion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a>, which is regarded as an etymological fiction. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> ravaged its territory in 211 BCE during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> but did not attack the city. According to Florus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> destroyed it as a punishment for loyalty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a>, though it is more probable that its lands were confiscated and given to his soldiers. In 49 BCE it was a populous town, and its citizens, favourable to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, opened their gates to his lieutenant M. Antonius. Ovid calls it a small provincial town, one of three municipal towns in the territory of the Paeligni.</p><p>Tradition places the beginning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in Sulmona in the 3rd century. Saint Pamphilus, a convert from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corfinium">Corfinium</a>, was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Valva in 682, died in 706 and is the patron saint of the town. Sulmona became a free commune under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a>, within the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> an aqueduct was built, and he made it the capital of a large province, with a tribunal and a fair, which it lost to the Angevins. A paper industry began in the 16th century, and an earthquake in 1706 nearly razed the city, which was largely rebuilt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sulmona is the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a>, who is commemorated by a bronze statue on the main road. Surviving medieval buildings include the Church of Santa Maria della Tomba, the Palazzo Annunziata, the aqueduct and the Gothic portal on Corso Ovidio. Campo 78, a prisoner-of-war camp, held Austrian prisoners in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and as many as 3,000 British and Commonwealth soldiers in the Second, until the guards deserted in September 1943.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sulmona” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their main towns were Corfinium, <b>Sulmo</b> and Superaequum, small hill communities that formed a league.</blockquote><a href="/state/paeligni">Paeligni</a></li></ul></section>
